; P0-item-4 (ORDER-SIGNIFICANT paths): two @key pragmas declaring the SAME
; ordered column path are a parse-time reject (each instance key must be a
; distinct ordered path). `@key(A) @key(A)` is an exact repeat of the one-column
; path [A], so it rejects regardless of order-significance. Rejects during
; PARSE, before demand. The bound query below matches {A}: it exists ONLY so
; that removing the dup-path parse check would flip this reject->accept (a LOST
; CHECK), i.e. the query makes the case actually GUARD the dup-path check.
; See reject_key_double_2 for the multi-column ordered-exact-dup guard, and note
; that `@key(A, B) @key(B, A)` is NOW ACCEPTED at parse (distinct paths) — the
; P0-item-4 order-significance flip.
#message b_1(u64 X, u64 Y).
#local foo(u64 A, u64 B) @key(A) @key(A).
foo(A, B) : b_1(A, B).
#query q(bound u64 A, free u64 B) : foo(A, B).
